  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    A half-flooded thermal cavern in the Nightshelf deep reaches, established as a fire-core mining site during the Frozen Peace, the period of reconstruction following the Orca Incursion. Once a reliable geothermal source, Iasnolica has degraded into something the miners themselves call the **Sick-Thaw** — a designation the official Nightshelf records do not use but cannot suppress.
    
    ## Physical Conditions
    
    The cavern floor is treacherous with half-melted ice shelves and unpredictable thermal vents. Work crews probe ahead with fire-hardened staves before each step. The water that pools here no longer steams correctly, and fire-cores extracted from its veins emerge brittle, prone to cold-cracking, often shot through with black veining consistent with Drift-coalesce (glacial), the uncontrolled aurora-crystallization corruption documented at several deep-Berg source points. The air carries a copper-and-char smell — not forge-heat but something smoldering in dark water.
    
    Deeper chambers are worse. Bioluminescent moss has died back entirely. The corruption there is dense enough to absorb echolocation, returning only smeared whisper-echoes — an acoustic property exploited during the Orca Incursion, when these corridors served as a practice ground for Gut Inversion, the counter-technique designed to kill orca raiders from the inside. The corridors reshape with each thermal cycle. During flares, exit channels can seal entirely behind walls of black water.
    
    Hush☽ Twilight, the Nightshelf silence-magic, and the Rupture-Song, the orca scarification war-ritual, both manifest here — a convergence that says something about what kind of place this has become.
    
    ## History
    
    The walls bear scorch-marks from Orca Incursion raids. Three penguin miners drowned during the wars; the ice preserves their impressions. Orca hunters Grul and Gork later infiltrated the cavern to destroy fire-cores powering Gut Inversion. Gork was killed by the Augur, a shadow-steel wielding defender. The cavern collapsed behind the surviving raider. An inscription near the main shaft — *Kzaith still burns* — has been wearing smooth under running water long enough that no one remembers who wrote it.
    
    ## Significance
    
    The Flipper Accord's fire-core quotas still require what Iasnolica produces. Crews come in shortened rotations, harvest what they can, and leave. The cavern's name has entered Nightshelf vernacular: *going to Iasnolica~* means drawing from a source you know is wrong because the alternative is nothing.
    
    The Drowned Ledge mining community preserves this usage in their oral proverbs. They add a coda the surface records omit: you do not go back unchanged.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
